{"networkAttributes":[{"KEGG_PATHWAY_IMAGE":"https://www.kegg.jp/kegg/pathway/spo/spo04011.png","KEGG_PATHWAY_ORG":"spo","modules_id":[""],"KEGG_PATHWAY_ID":"path:spo04011","classes":["Environmental Information Processing","Signal transduction"],"description":"The S. cerevisiae genome encodes multiple MAP kinase orthologs. One (Fus3) mediates cellular response to peptide pheromones. Another (Kss1) permits adjustment to nutrient limiting conditions. A third (Hog1) is necessary for survival under hyperosmotic conditions. A fourth (Slt2/Mpk1) is required for repair of injuries to the cell wall. As in mammalian cells, these pathways consist of a conserved module in which three kinases phosphorylate each other in sequence. The MAPK is phosphorylated by the MAPK/ERK kinase (MAPKK/MEK), which is itself phosphorylated by a MEK kinase (MAPKKK/MEKK).","diseases":[""],"diseases_id":[""],"KEGG_PATHWAY_LINK":"https://www.kegg.jp/kegg-bin/show_pathway?spo04011","modules":[""],"name":"MAPK signaling pathway - yeast [spo04011]_1"}]},
